<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David II of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1324">1324</a>–<a href="/years/1371">1371</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> II was King of Scots from 1329 to 1371, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>. Driven into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-balliol">Edward Balliol</a> and the English in 1334, he returned in 1341, but in 1346 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was defeated and captured at Neville's Cross and was held in England for eleven years. He was released in 1357 for a heavy ransom and died without children.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-ii-of-scotland.jpg" alt="David II of Scotland" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scotland_penny_802002_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> was born on 5 March 1324 at Dunfermline Abbey, one of twin sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> and Elizabeth de Burgh. His mother died in 1327. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-edinburgh-northampton">Treaty of Edinburgh-Northampton</a> he was married on 17 July 1328, aged four, to the seven-year-old Joan, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II of England</a> and Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Robert the Bruce died on 7 June 1329, and David became king at the age of five. He and Joan were crowned at Scone Abbey on 24 November 1331, and he was the first Scottish monarch to be anointed at his coronation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During his minority the kingdom was governed by guardians, several of whom fell in battle, among them Donald, Earl of Mar, at Dupplin Moor in August 1332 and Archibald Douglas at Halidon Hill in July 1333. After Dupplin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-balliol">Edward Balliol</a>, a protégé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, was crowned by the English, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-war-of-scottish-independence">Second War of Scottish Independence</a> began. After the English victory at Halidon Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Joan were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> on 14 May 1334. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on 2 June 1341, landing at Inverbervie, and took government into his own hands at the age of 17.</p><p>In 1346, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-alliance">Auld Alliance</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to draw Edward III away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Battle of Neville's Cross</a> on 17 October 1346, and he was wounded by two arrows to the face and taken prisoner. He remained in captivity in England for eleven years. By the Treaty of Berwick of 3 October 1357 the Scots agreed to pay 100,000 marks at 10,000 marks a year. When the instalment of 1363 could not be raised, David offered to bequeath the throne to Edward III or one of his sons, and in 1364 the Scottish parliament rejected a proposal to make Lionel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> the next king. From 1364 David governed actively and dealt firmly with defiant nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Queen Joan died on 7 September 1362. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> married Margaret Drummond in about February 1364 and tried to divorce her in 1370 because she had borne no children; Pope Urban V reversed the divorce. David died unexpectedly of natural causes at Edinburgh Castle on 22 February 1371, aged 46, and was buried before the high altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a>. As the last male of the House of Bruce, he was succeeded by his nephew Robert II. Despite long periods in exile and captivity, he had ensured the survival of his kingdom and left the monarchy in a strong position.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David II of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second War of Independence brought defeats at Halidon Hill in 1333 and Neville’s Cross in 1346, where <b>David II</b> was captured.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dunfermline Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Edinburgh Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.07,-3.47&amp;year=1354" aria-label="Dunfermline Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4475 255 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of David II of Scotland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4925 525h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4925 525h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.07,-3.47&amp;year=1354">What was here in 1354? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
